1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,395
[BRIGHT MUSIC]

2
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:09,727
INTERVIEWER: Welcome back
to, Are You Normalized?

3
00:00:09,727 --> 00:00:12,258
[APPLAUSE]

4
00:00:13,130 --> 00:00:15,860
Our final contestant
is Tina Tables

5
00:00:15,860 --> 00:00:18,330
from Dataville University.

6
00:00:18,330 --> 00:00:19,560
TINA TABLES: Hi, everyone.

7
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:22,010
I'd like to give a
shout out to my 3330

8
00:00:22,010 --> 00:00:25,313
classmates and my favorite
professor, Dr. Kelly.

9
00:00:25,313 --> 00:00:28,420
[APPLAUSE]

10
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:32,509
INTERVIEWER: That's
very nice, but I

11
00:00:32,509 --> 00:00:34,050
didn't approve that shout out.

12
00:00:34,050 --> 00:00:37,260
So we'll deduct a couple of
points right off the bat.

13
00:00:37,260 --> 00:00:40,340
[SOMBER MUSIC]

14
00:00:40,340 --> 00:00:43,500
All right, let's get normalized.

15
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:46,650
All right, Tina, here's
your final table design.

16
00:00:46,650 --> 00:00:49,460
This table consists
of three columns--

17
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:51,393
Course, Professor, and Textbook.

18
00:00:54,860 --> 00:01:00,200
As you can see, Course and
Professor are both underlined.

19
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:01,830
Are you ready, Tina?

20
00:01:01,830 --> 00:01:04,190
TINA TABLES: I'm ready, Freddy.

21
00:01:04,190 --> 00:01:05,670
INTERVIEWER: Who's Freddy?

22
00:01:05,670 --> 00:01:07,080
Is he a new player?

23
00:01:07,080 --> 00:01:10,550
Didn't authorize that either.

24
00:01:10,550 --> 00:01:13,110
Anyways, let's get started.

25
00:01:13,110 --> 00:01:16,860
Tina, is this table in
first normal form or not?

26
00:01:16,860 --> 00:01:17,910
TINA TABLES: Let's see.

27
00:01:17,910 --> 00:01:24,060
I use the D-R-O-P, DROP acronym,
to determine first normal form.

28
00:01:24,060 --> 00:01:27,800
The D stands for data
types are consistent,

29
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:33,620
R, no repeating groups, O, one
piece of information per cell,

30
00:01:33,620 --> 00:01:39,460
and P, primary key
is established.

31
00:01:39,460 --> 00:01:43,790
Hmm, in this case, I can only
determine that a primary key

32
00:01:43,790 --> 00:01:45,210
has been established.

33
00:01:45,210 --> 00:01:49,320
We have a composite primary
key with Course and Professor.

34
00:01:49,320 --> 00:01:54,650
So yes, it's in
first normal form.

35
00:01:54,650 --> 00:01:55,765
INTERVIEWER: You got it.

36
00:01:55,765 --> 00:01:56,458
[BELL CHIMES]

37
00:01:56,458 --> 00:01:58,130
[APPLAUSE]

38
00:01:58,130 --> 00:02:02,480
Round two, is the table
in second normal form?

39
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:04,550
TINA TABLES: We have a
composite primary key.

40
00:02:04,550 --> 00:02:07,360
So it's possible to have
a partial dependency

41
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:10,340
or second normal form violation.

42
00:02:10,340 --> 00:02:13,420
However, in this
case, Course alone

43
00:02:13,420 --> 00:02:16,453
doesn't determine the
textbook, and Professor alone

44
00:02:16,453 --> 00:02:17,745
doesn't determine the textbook.

45
00:02:20,736 --> 00:02:21,620
Yes.

46
00:02:21,620 --> 00:02:24,080
It's in second normal form.

47
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:25,863
INTERVIEWER: Absolutely correct.

48
00:02:25,863 --> 00:02:26,570
[BELL CHIMES]

49
00:02:26,570 --> 00:02:28,240
[APPLAUSE]

50
00:02:28,240 --> 00:02:32,330
Round 3, is the table
in third normal form?

51
00:02:32,330 --> 00:02:34,570
TINA TABLES: Third
normal form requires

52
00:02:34,570 --> 00:02:36,950
no transitive dependencies.

53
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:40,460
No non-key can determine
another non-key.

54
00:02:40,460 --> 00:02:42,800
There is only one non-key.

55
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:47,860
The table is in
third normal form.

56
00:02:47,860 --> 00:02:50,713
INTERVIEWER: Well, I've
determined that you are correct.

57
00:02:50,713 --> 00:02:51,500
[BELL CHIMES]

58
00:02:51,500 --> 00:02:53,367
[APPLAUSE]

59
00:02:53,367 --> 00:02:54,200
TINA TABLES: Yippee.

60
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:54,820
I did it.

61
00:02:54,820 --> 00:02:56,530
I win, right?

62
00:02:56,530 --> 00:02:57,710
Where are the streamers?

63
00:02:57,710 --> 00:02:59,860
Where's the grand prize?

64
00:02:59,860 --> 00:03:03,260
INTERVIEWER: Calm down, Tina,
or we will call security.

65
00:03:03,260 --> 00:03:03,960
All right.

66
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:07,310
[APPLAUSE]

67
00:03:08,290 --> 00:03:11,600
There is one final question
for the grand prize.

68
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:12,950
This one's a hard one.

69
00:03:12,950 --> 00:03:16,540
Is every determinant
a candidate key?

70
00:03:16,540 --> 00:03:17,920
TINA TABLES: Of course.

71
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:19,340
Wait.

72
00:03:19,340 --> 00:03:20,530
What?

73
00:03:20,530 --> 00:03:23,390
INTERVIEWER: It's an important
concept to normalization.

74
00:03:23,390 --> 00:03:25,310
Everyone should know this one.

75
00:03:25,310 --> 00:03:28,790
Is every determinant
a candidate key?

76
00:03:28,790 --> 00:03:29,800
TINA TABLES: Yes.

77
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:34,400
In this table, every
determinant is a candidate key.

78
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:36,980
INTERVIEWER: Oh,
I'm sorry, Tina.

79
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:40,695
In this table, every determinant
is not a candidate key.

80
00:03:40,695 --> 00:03:43,600
[SOMBER MUSIC]

81
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:46,230
Look closely at the columns,
Textbook and Course.

82
00:03:51,940 --> 00:03:54,520
TINA TABLES: Well,
based on the data,

83
00:03:54,520 --> 00:03:56,540
Textbook does determine Course.

84
00:03:56,540 --> 00:04:00,640
But I thought third normal
form was all there was.

85
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:02,210
INTERVIEWER: A common mistake.

86
00:04:02,210 --> 00:04:05,180
Typically, third normal
form is sufficient.

87
00:04:05,180 --> 00:04:09,620
However, on very rare occasions,
normalization problems persist.

88
00:04:09,620 --> 00:04:14,690
This table violates Boyce-Codd
normal form, or BCNF for short.

89
00:04:14,690 --> 00:04:18,230
That said, BCNF doesn't
occur very often,

90
00:04:18,230 --> 00:04:21,860
and it wasn't included in the
original paper on normalization.

91
00:04:21,860 --> 00:04:25,690
However, in our sample table,
we have a non-key attribute,

92
00:04:25,690 --> 00:04:29,290
Textbook, that determines part
of the composite primary key

93
00:04:29,290 --> 00:04:30,200
course.

94
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:33,260
That's what we call
a BCNF problem.

95
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:34,850
TINA TABLES: Oh, I see.

96
00:04:34,850 --> 00:04:37,100
With Textbook
determining Course,

97
00:04:37,100 --> 00:04:39,860
then not all determinants
are candidate keys,

98
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:43,390
even though the table is in the
first, second, and third normal

99
00:04:43,390 --> 00:04:44,120
form.

100
00:04:44,120 --> 00:04:49,690
Textbook determines
part of the primary key.

101
00:04:49,690 --> 00:04:50,620
INTERVIEWER: Exactly.

102
00:04:50,620 --> 00:04:52,400
That's BCNF for you.

103
00:04:52,400 --> 00:04:54,383
Let's fix the BCNF problem.

104
00:05:06,377 --> 00:05:08,830
[APPLAUSE]

105
00:05:08,830 --> 00:05:11,770
First, we create a new
table with, Textbook,

106
00:05:11,770 --> 00:05:15,470
as the primary key and, Course,
as the non-key attribute.

107
00:05:15,470 --> 00:05:18,670
Then we remove the Course
column from the original table

108
00:05:18,670 --> 00:05:22,280
and make Textbook the foreign
key link to the new table.

109
00:05:22,280 --> 00:05:25,270
In the original table, we
make Professor and Textbook

110
00:05:25,270 --> 00:05:27,020
composite primary keys.

111
00:05:27,020 --> 00:05:29,210
Now, every determinant is a key.

112
00:05:29,210 --> 00:05:30,363
BCNF clean.

113
00:05:40,660 --> 00:05:43,400
TINA TABLES: Wait,
this is the fix?

114
00:05:43,400 --> 00:05:46,240
That design looks terrible.

115
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:48,940
INTERVIEWER: The
design is in BCNF.

116
00:05:48,940 --> 00:05:52,220
TINA TABLES: Even though
you're squeaky clean and BCNF,

117
00:05:52,220 --> 00:05:55,320
this whole design would be
better with a surrogate key.

118
00:06:11,280 --> 00:06:14,650
Sometimes the best key
isn't natural at all.

119
00:06:14,650 --> 00:06:15,840
It's a surrogate.

120
00:06:15,840 --> 00:06:22,560
One column, identity 1, 1.

121
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:26,020
Simple joins, no
messy composites.

122
00:06:26,020 --> 00:06:29,380
And your functional
dependencies stay clear.

123
00:06:29,380 --> 00:06:30,480
INTERVIEWER: Wow.

124
00:06:30,480 --> 00:06:31,685
You are correct.

125
00:06:31,685 --> 00:06:33,600
[APPLAUSE]

126
00:06:33,600 --> 00:06:35,980
I guess normalization
gets you far,

127
00:06:35,980 --> 00:06:39,660
but design is about clarity,
and sometimes the real winner

128
00:06:39,660 --> 00:06:41,290
is a surrogate key.

129
00:06:41,290 --> 00:06:43,540
TINA TABLES: So did I
win the grand prize?

130
00:06:43,540 --> 00:06:45,120
INTERVIEWER: Absolutely.

131
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:48,566
The grand prize is knowledge.

132
00:06:48,566 --> 00:06:50,580
[APPLAUSE]

133
00:06:50,580 --> 00:06:53,130
Congratulations, Tina.